The lowly PDF is the cockroach of file formats—annoying, unstoppable, often despised and but still more future-proof than half the web. I can open a PDF from [from 1996](https://web.archive.org/web/20051103044315/http://www.pdf-tools.com/public/downloads/pdf-reference/pdfreference12.pdf) more reliably than a mid-2000s Flash microsite.

But PDFs are still not great, they bring a range of issues and, at a minimum, present users with an annoying choice: read the the web page with added context and related materials or the PDF download. Better to be able to have both.

In my daily work, this issue has become more profound: On [PreventionWeb.net](https://PreventionWeb.net), research papers and guidance docs *are* the product, so the hand-off between “nice web page” and “raw download” matters. Right now we force people to pick one. Why not serve both?

## Why is *this* still a problem?

It’s 2025. We have rockets that land themselves. Yet most “drop-in” PDF viewers are:

You'd think, in 2025, that there'd be a drop-in, lightweight, accessible PDF viewer that just works. But after poking around, most solutions are either heavy, locked behind paywalls, or just not quite right for our needs. I want something that:

- Loads quickly
- Easy UX metaphor for mobile and desktop
- Is accessible (keyboard navigation, ARIA, screen reader support)
- Lets us customize the UI (show/hide controls, tweak the look)
- Supports deep linking and shareable page URLs
- Some search support
- Doesn't drag in a pile of dependencies


## Enter PDF-A-go-go

While there are 3rd party tools for this, such as embedding the raw PDF viewer or commercial services: these are either clunky, inconsistent, inflexible or just not great.

PDF-A-go-go is a drop‑in PDF viewer you can embed with a single script tag. Add a tiny CSS file for polish, set options with `data-*` attributes, and you’re done—no framework, no build step, no init code. It works almost anywhere you can write HTML.

### Why PDF‑A‑go‑go?

- **One‑line install**: Include one JS file, optionally one small CSS file
- **Tiny footprint**: Real‑world demo transfer ~120 KB core viewer JS, ~490 KB JS dependencies, and ~4 KB CSS
- **Works almost anywhere**: Static HTML, CMS templates, site builders, SSGs; no `<iframe>` required
- **Super flexible embed**: Configure everything via `data-*` attributes—no custom JS required
- **Accessible and fast**: Keyboard navigation, ARIA labels, screen‑reader support, and performance‑minded rendering

Instead of bending yet another third-party widget to our will, I rolled a tiny wrapper around Mozilla’s excellent [PDF.js](https://mozilla.github.io/pdf.js/). It keeps the good bits, bins the bloat and adds a few essentials:

- **Side-scroll reading** – more magazine, less micro‑fiche
- **Show/hide controls via data-attributes** – search, share, page selector, current page, download, fullscreen, resize grip, and visible accessibility instructions
- **Full‑text search** – with highlighting and next/previous match navigation
- **Shareable page links** – deep links via URL fragments
- **Keyboard navigation** – comprehensive shortcuts across zoom, navigation, and search
- **Resizable viewer** – drag handle to adjust height
- **Download + Fullscreen** – built-in controls
- **Smart HTML download handling** – works with institutional repositories that gate PDFs behind an HTML step
- **Lightweight + embeddable** – single script tag + small CSS, no framework required
- **Built-in accessibility** – ARIA roles, focus management, screen reader friendly
- **Performance tuned** – progressive render and cleanup on top of PDF.js

Integration is as simple as dropping in a JS and CSS file, then adding a container div with a few data attributes. No React, no build step, no fuss. Here's what it looks like in practice:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://khawkins98.github.io/PDF-A-go-go/pdf-a-go-go.css">
<script defer src="https://khawkins98.github.io/PDF-A-go-go/pdf-a-go-go.js"></script>

<div class="pdfagogo-container" id="pdfagogo-container"
     data-pdf-url="./example.pdf"
     data-show-search="true"
     data-show-share="true"
     data-show-page-selector="true"
     data-show-current-page="true"
     data-show-download="true"
     data-show-fullscreen="true"
     data-show-resize-grip="true"
     data-show-accessibility-controls-visibly="true"
     style="width:100%;max-width:100%;box-sizing:border-box;"></div>
```

Want to hide the download button? Just set `data-show-download="false"`. Need to start on page 4? Add `data-default-page="4"`. It's all in the markup.

Here's a live example of PDF-A-go-go in action. For more info or to contribute, check out the project on <a href="https://github.com/khawkins98/PDF-A-go-go" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> and see the <a href="https://khawkins98.github.io/PDF-A-go-go/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">live demo</a>.

